From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 27 May 2026 15:01:43 -0700
Subject: [PATCH RFC 0/3] net: flow_dissector: straight-line fast-path for common L3+L4 shapes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

This RFC adds a straight-line fast-path entry to
__skb_flow_dissect() for the most common packet shapes seen on
modern RX paths: ethernet + {IPv4, IPv6} + {TCP, UDP}. On a hit,
the fast-path produces byte-identical struct flow_keys output to
the slow path and returns without entering the generic parse-graph
state machine.

This is my third small kernel submission. The two prior RFCs
(flow_hash_from_keys_small + sch_cake adoption; bpf_flow PPPoE)
came out of the same XDP2-vs-kernel comparison work led by Tom
Herbert (https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2). This series is
the **largest single performance technique** from that comparison
work that's portable to the in-tree dissector — and the most
substantive of the three submissions to date.

Background — same comparison work, different lever
==================================================

XDP2's generated mono parser runs 35-42 % faster than
__skb_flow_dissect_err on common L2-L4 traffic. Attributing
that delta to its component techniques (XDP2 commit history
R3-R8, https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2):

  Phase                  | https-web ns/pkt | delta
  -----------------------|------------------|------
  Pre-R3.3               | 116              | -
  R3.3 IR-coverage devirt| 116              | 0 (null)
  R3.4 fast-path chains  | 71               | -45 (-39 %)
  R5/R6/R7 polish        | 72               | +1 (noise)
  R8 VXLAN fast-path     | 72               | 0 on flat
                                              workloads

**One technique — R3.4 "fast-path chains at parser entry" — is
essentially the entire flat-workload speedup.** R3.3 (explicit
devirtualisation of indirect calls) was a null because gcc -O3
+ LTO already inlines indirect calls at compile time when the
target is constant — the compiler does it for free.

This series backports that R3.4 technique to the in-tree C
dissector. The technique is portable straight-line C — no
codegen, no LTO requirement, no new tooling, no new ABI. The
implementation is ~280 lines of straight-line C across three
patches.

The patches
===========

  1/3  flow_dissector: add fast-path entry-point skeleton
       (~96 LoC, framework only, no behaviour change)

  2/3  flow_dissector: add eth+IPv4+{TCP,UDP} fast-path
       (~88 LoC, plugs IPv4 stub from patch 1)

  3/3  flow_dissector: add eth+IPv6+{TCP,UDP} fast-path
       (~101 LoC, plugs IPv6 stub from patch 1)

The framework patch (1/3) establishes:

  - The dispatch site: in __skb_flow_dissect() AFTER the BPF
    flow_dissector override check, BEFORE the slow-path graph
    walk. BPF semantics unchanged.
  - Dissector identity check: only the two standard dissectors
    (flow_keys_dissector, flow_keys_dissector_symmetric). Custom
    dissectors (e.g. tc cls_flow with bespoke key sets) defer
    unconditionally.
  - Flag-rejection: any flag combination other than the empty
    set or FLOW_DISSECTOR_F_PARSE_1ST_FRAG defers (we don't
    implement encap walking, MPLS, flow-label stop, etc.).
  - Fall-through cost on a fast-path miss: one ethertype switch
    (~5 cycles). Independent of how many fast-path shapes are
    wired in.

Patches 2 and 3 are mechanical applications of the framework
to the two address families. Each rejects IP options
(IPv4 IHL != 5), fragments, extension headers (IPv6), and
non-{TCP,UDP} transports. Each writes the same key set the slow
path would for the standard dissector (CONTROL.{addr_type,
thoff, flags}, BASIC.{n_proto, ip_proto}, IPV{4,6}_ADDRS,
PORTS).

What this is NOT
================

- NOT a new dissector API. __skb_flow_dissect() is unchanged from
  the caller's perspective.
- NOT a new struct flow_keys layout. The struct is ABI-affecting
  (BPF flow_dissector hook contract); this series doesn't touch
  it.
- NOT a change in BPF flow_dissector hook semantics. The
  fast-path runs only after the existing BPF override check
  decides no BPF program is loaded for this netns.
- NOT bit-exact with any specific RSS / RFS / multipath hash. The
  fast-path produces the same struct flow_keys; downstream hash
  functions remain unchanged.
- NOT VLAN-aware (yet). VLAN-tagged packets defer to the slow
  path. A VLAN fast-path is a natural follow-up; held back from
  v1 to keep the review surface small.
- NOT an XDP2 framework upstream attempt. The XDP2 framework
  itself is a separate, larger conversation
  (https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2,
  formerly PANDA, see "Replacing Flow Dissector with PANDA
  Parser" at Netdev 0x15
  https://netdevconf.info/0x15/slides/16/Flow%20dissector_PANDA%20parser.pdf
  ). This series captures the largest single technique
  from that work in hand-written kernel C; no framework
  required.

Why the kernel can't capture ALL of XDP2's win
==============================================

XDP2's mono parser at 71 ns/pkt has three properties this
in-tree C can't replicate:

  1. always_inline + LTO into the caller's translation unit:
     XDP2's parser inlines into the benchmark's parse loop as
     one ~10 KB block. The kernel's __skb_flow_dissect() is a
     generic API; flow_dissector.o and sch_cake.o are different
     translation units, no LTO, function too large to inline.
     Cost: ~5-10 ns/pkt of function-call / register-save
     overhead.

  2. Constant-folding of static const proto_def fields: XDP2
     mono parser folds protocol-table constants at compile time.
     The kernel reads runtime structs. Cost: ~2-5 ns/pkt.

  3. Per-callsite specialisation: XDP2 produces a fresh parser
     per use-site if desired. Kernel flow_dissector is one
     function serving many callers. Cost: 0-3 ns/pkt on the
     common path.

Total un-recoverable structural gap: ~10-15 ns/pkt. The kernel
captures the dispatch-loop bypass (the bulk of the win) and pays
the inherent generic-API tax for the rest. **Expected kernel
result: 117 -> ~85-90 ns/pkt on https-web, ~25-30 % faster vs
today.** Still substantial; absolutely worth shipping
incrementally.

Output byte-equivalence
=======================

The fast-path is required to produce byte-identical
target_container output to the slow path on every shape it
handles, on the standard dissectors. The architectural contract
is enforced by careful field-by-field replication of the slow
path's writes:

  Slow path:                       Fast-path:
  ----------                       ----------
  v4: key_addrs->v4addrs.{src,dst} same memcpy
      key_control->addr_type        same (= IPV4_ADDRS)
  out: key_control->thoff           same (= nhoff + 20)
       key_basic->n_proto           same (= htons(ETH_P_IP))
       key_basic->ip_proto          same (iph->protocol)
  ports: skb_flow_get_ports         direct __be32 read at thoff+0
                                    (proto_ports_offset(TCP/UDP)
                                    is 0 for both)

Cold key slots (KEY_IP TOS/TTL, KEY_TCP flags, KEY_VLAN, etc.)
are not in flow_keys_dissector's request list and stay at the
caller's pre-memset zero — same as the slow path's behaviour for
the standard dissector on this packet shape.

The IPv6 path additionally defers when the dissector requests
FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_FLOW_LABEL and the packet carries a non-zero
label, since the slow path writes key_tags->flow_label in that
case. Zero-labelled packets (the vast majority of real traffic)
proceed through the fast-path normally.

Pre-post checklist done
=======================

Static:

  - net/core/flow_dissector.c W=1 compile clean for each patch.
  - scripts/checkpatch.pl --strict: 0 errors, 0 warnings, 0
    checks for each of the three patches.
  - The framework patch's dispatch site is the only outwardly
    visible change in __skb_flow_dissect(); the three new helper
    functions are file-static and don't appear in any header.

Pre-post checklist still TODO before non-RFC posting
====================================================

- Kernel selftest under tools/testing/selftests/net/ that
  exercises both the fast-path and the slow-path on a packet
  corpus (synthetic + the kernel's existing pcap test set) and
  compares struct flow_keys byte-for-byte for every packet.
  Held out of v1 because the toggle mechanism (sysctl vs
  static_key vs CONFIG) deserves separate maintainer feedback.
- Per-workload microbench numbers on a multi-uarch testbed.
  Initial measurement on Zen 1 (Ryzen 5 PRO 2400G) via the
  userspace harness at
  github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2/samples/flow_dissector/
  shows the expected ~25-30 % range, but a kernel selftest +
  internal numbers would be stronger evidence.
- 24-hour soak test on patched kernel under sustained iperf3
  load through sch_cake on the four-host benchmark testbed
  used for the prior two RFCs. Same methodology as
  perf-results/2026-05-25/soak-B1-iperf3-24h-results.md.
- VLAN fast-path (eth+VLAN+IPv4+{TCP,UDP} and eth+VLAN+IPv6+
  {TCP,UDP}). Adds ~150 LoC and an ETH_P_8021Q dispatch arm.
  Held for v2 once the v1 design lands.

This is sent as RFC for early feedback on:

  - The dispatch shape (single ethertype switch with per-family
    extractors). Reviewers may prefer a different organisation.
  - The byte-exact contract (vs. allowing the fast-path to skip
    "cold" slot writes that the consumer-touch matrix shows are
    unread). Skipping cold writes saves ~3 ns/pkt but adds
    forward-compat risk if a future consumer reads them.
  - The dissector-identity check (only flow_keys_dissector and
    flow_keys_dissector_symmetric). A broader policy (e.g. any
    dissector whose key set is a subset of what the fast-path
    writes) might catch more callers.
  - Whether a CONFIG knob / static_key / sysctl is wanted for
    runtime A/B testing. v1 omits all toggles.

Detailed design + per-mechanism analysis at
github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2/docs/kernel-flowdis-fastpath-plan.md
on the same branch as the prior two RFCs.

